Learning outcomes
By the end of this lesson, you should be able to:

explain why decision theory is useful when future outcomes are not fully known;
distinguish a decision alternative from a state of nature;
identify the consequence associated with an action-state combination;
construct and interpret a simple payoff or loss table; and
describe the four basic steps in the decision-theory approach.


Week 1 focus. Decision problems, alternatives, states of nature, consequences, payoff/loss tables, and the basic steps in the decision-theory approach.

3.3 Consequences
A consequence is the result obtained when a particular alternative is chosen and a particular state of nature occurs.
The consequence might be expressed as:

runs conceded;
probability of winning;
points won;
race time;
expected medal probability;
injury risk;
expected performance score;
or another quantity relevant to the decision.

We denote the consequence of choosing alternative (A_i) when state (S_j) occurs by (C_{ij}).
For example:

(A_2): Spin bowler
(S_1): Batter attacks aggressively

Then (C_{21}) is the consequence of using the spinner when the batter attacks aggressively.

Learning outcomes
By the end of this lesson, you should be able to:

distinguish decision making under certainty, uncertainty, and risk;
identify the information available in each environment;
formulate a statistical decision problem using alternatives, states of nature, consequences, and available information;
classify a sports decision problem into the appropriate decision-making environment; and
explain why the choice of decision rule depends on the information available.


Week 2 focus. Decisions under certainty, uncertainty, and risk, together with the formulation of statistical decision problems.

Definition
A decision is made under risk when:

more than one state of nature is possible; and
probabilities can be assigned to the states.

The probabilities may come from:

historical data;
statistical models;
expert judgement;
or a combination of these.

7.1 Decision maker
Who chooses the action?
Examples:

captain;
coach;
athlete;
selection committee.

Mistake 3: Ignoring the consequence measure
If the consequence is:

race time, lower is better;
runs conceded, lower is better;
win probability, higher is better;
expected points, higher is better.

The direction of preference must be clear before applying a decision rule.

